   "text": "# The Printing Press: A History of the Machine That Remade the World\n\nFew inventions in human history have exerted as profound and far-reaching an influence as the printing press. Before its emergence in fifteenth-century Europe, the written word was the preserve of a small elite\u2014scribes, scholars, clerics, and the wealthy\u2014who could afford the enormous expense and time required to produce a single manuscript. Books were luxury objects, laboriously copied by hand over months or years, and libraries were treasures guarded jealously by institutions. The printing press shattered this monopoly. By mechanizing the reproduction of text, it made ideas portable, affordable, and abundant, setting in motion transformations in religion, science, politics, education, and culture whose effects are still unfolding today. To trace the history of the printing press is, in many ways, to trace the history of modernity itself.\n\n## Precursors: Printing Before Gutenberg\n\nThe story of the printing press does not begin abruptly in the 1440s. It is the culmination of centuries of technological and cultural development, drawing on traditions from China, Korea, the Islamic world, and medieval Europe. The earliest known form of printing is woodblock printing, which emerged in China during the Tang dynasty, around the seventh or eighth century",
